---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StudyHeader from "../../components/study/StudyHeader.astro";
import Act from "../../components/study/Act.astro";
import DecisionCard from "../../components/study/DecisionCard.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/study/Figure.astro";
import CapturePlaceholder from "../../components/study/CapturePlaceholder.astro";
import EndBlock from "../../components/study/EndBlock.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import screenReceiving from "../../assets/tpl/screen-01-receiving.png";
import screenHandoff from "../../assets/tpl/screen-02-handoff.png";
import screenIntake from "../../assets/tpl/screen-03-intake.png";
import "../../styles/study.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SusTern TPL Platform — one decision at a time — Danilo Boquet"
  description="Redesigning third-party logistics operations: one review surface per record, an exception-first queue, and one locked vocabulary across a provider workspace."
>
  <StudyHeader
    num="02"
    title="SusTern TPL Platform"
    hook="Third-party logistics is a queue of exceptions. My design goal: one home per record, one decision per row, and a queue that tells you what to do next."
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Product & UX design lead" },
      { label: "Company", value: "SusTern" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "2025 — present" },
      { label: "Scope", value: "Intake, receiving, dispatch, billing, design system" },
    ]}
    outcome="Four review surfaces → one. One decision per row. No new backend endpoints. Two confirmations — deliberately placed."
  />

  <Act num="01" title="The scene: a stack of documents, one question each">
    <p class="lede">
      It's morning at a third-party logistics provider. The day starts with a stack: packing
      lists, bills of lading, assemblies, return authorizations. Every document needs a call —
      accept it, correct it, chase it, or block it.
    </p>
    <p>
      When I took over the design, that work lived in four overlapping review surfaces: a row
      expansion, a facts modal, and two alternate views of the same extracted data. Operators had
      learned four patterns to do one thing. The vocabulary drifted too — the same state wore a
      different name on the nav, in the filter, and on the button.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tools worked. The design didn't. The rebuild answered one question: what does a
      provider see when the software is the calmest, most organized colleague on the floor? The
      TPL Platform is derived from the AI-Commerce platform, and it inherits the same principles:
      scoped context, evidence at hand, and one human decision at every commit.
    </p>
  </Act>

  <Act num="02" title="The constraint: providers run real operations">
    <ul class="constraints">
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-merchant by default.</strong>
        <span>
          A provider serves many client merchant workspaces at once. Every screen has to make its
          scope obvious — which client, which facility, which grant.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence or it didn't happen.</strong>
        <span>
          These records move money and goods. Every decision keeps its paperwork attached, and
          irreversible actions are staged and confirmed.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operators, not analysts.</strong>
        <span>
          Small teams in the middle of physical work. The queue has to tell them what's next
          without a manual.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Act>

  <Act num="03" title="The decisions">
    <DecisionCard
      title="One review surface per record"
      bet="A record opens in exactly one place: a fullscreen review with the decision state up top, evidence one reveal away, and provenance available but never in the way. Rows keep only a status chip, a reason line, and one action."
      alternative="Keep the four surfaces — a row expansion, a draft-facts modal, and alternate views of the same record."
      why="One place to act removes the oldest question in the room — “is this the same thing?” — and one pattern is learnable at a glance. Operators learn a decision, not a navigation."
    />
    <DecisionCard
      title="Exception-first queue with deterministic order"
      bet="The queue sorts by risk, not by time: conflicts first, then missing information, then needs review — and ready records fold quietly behind a “show ready” toggle. Every row derives exactly one action from its status."
      alternative="A chronological list where operators scan titles looking for problems."
      why="Attention is the scarce resource. When the queue orders itself by consequence, the workflow is the screen — nothing to memorize, nothing to hunt."
    />
    <DecisionCard
      title="Upload first, classify later — in one vocabulary"
      bet="A single upload surface stages documents before anything is classified; the role can be confirmed, corrected, or deferred. And one user-facing term per concept, locked — across nav, filters, buttons, and help."
      alternative="A wizard that interrogates you before accepting a file, with per-surface naming that drifts apart."
      why="People arrive holding a file and knowing why. Blocking the upload on classification slows the one thing they came to do — and terminology discipline is what makes a screen full of exceptions feel small."
    />
    <DecisionCard
      title="One workspace, scoped to context"
      bet="A single admin shell where you select the client namespace and the facility — and see only what your scope allows. Requirements appear as guidance (“requires a namespace, facility, and active grant”), not as disabled walls."
      alternative="Separate tools or skins per persona — provider, merchant, manufacturer."
      why="A small team can't maintain parallel products. One design language means one pattern library, one set of fixes, and screens that stay simple because context — not extra code — decides what's shown."
    />
    <div class="rejected">
      <p class="rejected-head">What we said no to</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A second upload path — one intake surface, no exceptions</li>
        <li>Four ways to review one record — one surface, one decision</li>
        <li>A confirmation on everything — we confirmed exactly two money actions</li>
        <li>A chronological queue — attention follows risk, not arrival time</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Act>

  <Act num="04" title="The work">
    <p class="study-disclosure">
      Real product captures below are labelled “Sanitized capture” (provider demo workspace);
      composed figures are labelled “Reconstruction.”
    </p>
    <div class="fig-grid">
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="The consolidated workspace: source documents, evidence, and domain handoffs in one place. Scope requirements appear as guidance — namespace, facility, grant — instead of disabled walls."
        pad={false}
      >
        <Image
          src={screenIntake}
          alt="Document intake workspace with four handoff cards and an upload panel with namespace, facility, role, and file fields"
          widths={[640, 960, 1356]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 1040px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="Consolidation made visible — the old source-intake tab now hands operators to the single workspace instead of keeping a second upload path alive."
        pad={false}
      >
        <Image
          src={screenHandoff}
          alt="Source intake tab showing a notice that uploads and reviews moved to the document intake workspace"
          widths={[640, 960, 1348]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 1040px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
      <Figure
        label="Sanitized capture"
        caption="The operational side of Inbound — receiving, returns and grading, and source intake sharing one surface and one vocabulary."
        pad={false}
      >
        <Image
          src={screenReceiving}
          alt="Receiving form with reference number, line items, lot and condition fields, and a confirm receipt action"
          widths={[640, 960, 1356]}
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 92vw, 1040px"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </Figure>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-grid">
      <CapturePlaceholder
        code="#C1"
        caption="The exception-first queue: status chips, one reason line, one action per row, “show ready” folded away. Completes the before → after pair with the handoff capture above."
      />
      <CapturePlaceholder
        code="#C3"
        caption="Commercial billing after the redesign: one row, one derived action, confirmations only where money and irreversibility meet."
      />
    </div>
    <div class="fig-grid fig-grid--2">
      <CapturePlaceholder
        code="#C2"
        caption="The review surface: decide first, evidence one reveal away, one primary action in the footer."
      />
      <Figure
        label="Reconstruction"
        caption="One term per concept, locked across every surface — the quiet work that makes the loud screens simple."
      >
        <div class="vocab">
          <div class="vocab-col vocab-dim">
            <h4>Before</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Row expansion — “processed”</li>
              <li>Facts modal — “in progress”</li>
              <li>Alternate view A — “pending review”</li>
              <li>Alternate view B — “needs checking”</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="vocab-col">
            <h4>After</h4>
            <ul>
              <li>Processing</li>
              <li>Needs review</li>
              <li>Conflicts</li>
              <li>Missing</li>
              <li>Ready</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Figure>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-grid fig-grid--2">
      <CapturePlaceholder
        code="#C4"
        caption="The calm end of the queue: nothing to chase. “Attention-first” works because it can also be quiet."
      />
      <CapturePlaceholder
        code="#C5"
        caption="The provider workspace on a narrow screen — one decision at a time survives the phone."
      />
    </div>
    <p class="group-label">How this shipped</p>
    <div class="shipped">
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Audit</span>
        <p>11 friction points catalogued before any screen changed</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Decide</span>
        <p>One review surface per record; alternatives recorded and rejected</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Ship</span>
        <p>Phased delivery, each step behind acceptance gates</p>
      </div>
      <div class="shipped-step">
        <span class="shipped-step-label">Govern</span>
        <p>Patterns moved into the design system; guardrails run in CI</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Act>

  <Act num="05" title="Impact & status">
    <p>
      Recently shipped — and I'll be precise about that. The four review surfaces became one; the
      queue leads with exceptions and derives one action per row; intake terminology is locked
      across the workspace. Commercial billing went from a hand-rolled page to a governed flow
      with confirmations on every irreversible step. And the platform's design system now ships
      in versioned releases with automated guardrail tests, so the patterns hold across a
      workspace that spans intake, receiving, dispatch, inventory, assembly, billing, and returns.
    </p>
    <div class="counted">
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">4 → 1</span>
        <span class="counted-label">review surfaces consolidated</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">11</span>
        <span class="counted-label">friction points resolved</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">0</span>
        <span class="counted-label">new backend endpoints required</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">2</span>
        <span class="counted-label">confirmations, deliberately placed</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">Green</span>
        <span class="counted-label">full suite, guardrails included</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      What doesn't exist yet is operator outcome data: the newest flows shipped weeks ago, and
      I'd rather show you the structure than a number I can't defend. The structure is checkable
      in the screenshots above.
    </p>
  </Act>

  <Act num="06" title="What I'd do differently">
    <p>
      I'd lock terminology in week one. Doing it at consolidation meant re-teaching every screen
      that already existed.
    </p>
    <p>
      And “show ready” should have been the default fold from the start. We knew attention should
      follow risk, but keeping ready records visible felt safer to stakeholders. The operators
      never needed them. Attention-first is the right default — everywhere.
    </p>
  </Act>

  <EndBlock nextTitle="noryX — the first product" nextHref="/work/noryx/" />
</BaseLayout>
